Raveshaw boss: liveness check for the thrown object.
ThrownObject is a raw SimpleGameObj pointer, read every think while
Raveshaw walks to the object (MOVE_STATE_GOTO_THROW_OBJECT), holds it
(THROWN_OBJECT_STATE_PICKUP) and throws it (THROWN_OBJECT_STATE_FLYING).
If anything other than the throw itself destroys it, the next think reads
freed memory, and a save writes a stale pointer that load could remap onto
an unrelated object. The retail "(Raveshaw Ammo)" presets (AC Unit
81960250, Plastic Drum 81960251; health 1000, skin and shield Blamo) take
damage only from BlamoKiller warheads (Ammo_SAM_Site_Blamo_Killer,
Ammo_UltimateWeapon). Retail M08 also places none of them: m08.ldd, the
M08.mix overlays, cinematics and scripts never name or reference these
definition IDs, so Find_Object_To_Throw returns NULL and the throwing state
always falls back to OVERALL_STATE_CHASE_STAR. The guard is defensive
(modified data, debug weapons, scripts) and changes nothing on retail data.

Add a GameObjReference (cleared by the engine when the object is destroyed)
next to the pointer and drop the pointer when the reference is gone, at the
start of each think and before saving. The walk-to state then takes the
original nothing-to-throw branch (OVERALL_STATE_CHASE_STAR); a grab or throw
already under way finishes empty-handed (PICKUP and the hand link were
already NULL-safe; FLYING now is too) and decides its next state as before.
Find_Object_To_Throw also skips delete-pending objects (freed by the next
NetworkObjectMgrClass::Delete_Pending), as GameObjManager's own lookups do;
no caller in the think order can select one today. The save format is
unchanged: the pointer is still saved and remapped, and On_Post_Load rebuilds
the reference from it. Breadcrumb (at most 4 per run): "A3.8 Raveshaw thrown
object: destroyed before landing; dropped stale pointer".

